10:11:24 | INFO | 🤖 Target: It can definitely *look* that way, but a fish approaching or inspecting the castle is not proof that it has forgotten it. Goldfish often revisit objects because of changing light, reflections, water movement, food residue, territorial/exploratory behavior, or simply because the object looks different from a new angle. Fish also don’t display “surprise” in a way we can reliably distinguish from curiosity, caution, or normal investigation. More importantly, memory is not the same as constantly paying attention. You can know a chair is in your room and still notice it again when you walk in, especially if something about it looks different. A goldfish may remember the castle as part of its environment while still swimming up to inspect it repeatedly. Experiments testing behavior over time—not just apparent reactions—show goldfish can learn routes, recognize feeding cues, and retain associations well beyond a few seconds. The cartoon version exaggerates a real-looking behavior into “it forgets everything immediately.”
