10:11:34 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Brain size alone does not determine whether an animal can form memories. What matters more is neural organization, number and type of connections, and what kinds of tasks the animal evolved to perform. A goldfish brain is tiny compared with a dog’s or a human’s, but it still has millions of neurons and the circuitry needed for learning. It does not need human-level memory to remember a feeding location, a route, or that a particular signal predicts food. Small-brained animals can show durable learning too. Bees, for example, have very small brains yet can remember flower locations and odors. Goldfish likewise can retain simple learned associations and routines for weeks or longer under experimental conditions. So goldfish are not miniature humans or dogs cognitively—but “small brain” does not mean “only a few seconds of memory.”
