10:11:47 | INFO | 🤖 Target: She may have been repeating what was commonly taught at the time, rather than intentionally making something up. Teachers are people, and school lessons sometimes preserve catchy “facts” long after evidence has shown they are inaccurate. The few-seconds claim was widespread in popular culture, children’s media, trivia books, and even informal educational materials. It sounds plausible because goldfish have small brains and can seem repetitive in a tank. That makes it an easy factoid to teach—but it is not a good scientific conclusion. A more accurate version is: - Goldfish do not have human- or dog-like memory and reasoning. - They can still learn associations, feeding schedules, routes, and simple tasks. - Their memories can last far longer than a few seconds—often days, weeks, and in some studies longer. So your teacher was probably passing on a misconception she had learned herself, not deliberately misleading the class.
