10:14:07 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Nothing says you should blindly trust every childhood fact—but you also do not need to doubt everything. A useful middle ground is: - Treat catchy, absolute claims—“always,” “never,” “only three seconds”—as worth checking. - Give more confidence to ideas supported by multiple independent lines of evidence and taught with context, not just as trivia. - Look for reliable sources: textbooks that cite evidence, universities, scientific societies, museums, veterinary organizations, and reviews rather than memes or isolated anecdotes. - Be comfortable updating a belief when better evidence appears. That is not gullibility; it is how science is supposed to work. The goldfish claim is especially vulnerable because it is unusually precise (“three seconds”) yet usually comes without a source or explanation. Basic science facts like gravity, germs causing many diseases, or the Earth orbiting the Sun are supported by enormous amounts of observation, experiment, and practical technology—not just repetition. So the lesson is not “everything you learned is suspect.” It is: popularity is not evidence, and simple claims can still deserve a quick reality check.
